WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.776
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.124 --> 00:00:11.600
JOHN VEIZADES: Good
morning, everyone.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.450
Thank you for sticking around
through the last day of Google

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:17.062
Next.

00:00:17.062 --> 00:00:18.520
And today we're
going to be talking

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:22.480
about how you can take
your enterprise network

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.370
and extend it out
into the Cloud,

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:28.000
various ways of doing that,
talking about reliability

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.900
of those mechanisms, and
talking about many use cases

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:34.300
on how you do that.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:35.485
So let's start off.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:43.150
So you've all seen this picture
or a picture much like it.

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:47.500
And this is the Google
backbone, and the network

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:49.330
that you can connect to.

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:52.000
So one of the things we
like to emphasize here

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.680
is that when you
make connections

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:58.060
to your VPC in Cloud,
you really are connecting

00:00:58.060 --> 00:00:59.920
to this infrastructure.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.100
So if you think about
the kind of connections

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.599
we're going to be talking about
today, which are connections

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:08.170
from your enterprise
into cloud, it's also

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:10.720
connections into this
backbone, and it's a way

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:11.860
to leverage that backbone.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:18.300
So there's a number of ways
you can connect to Google.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.150
I'm going to talk
briefly about two methods

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:24.000
that I'm not going to talk in
the rest of the deck about.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.170
One of them is direct and the
other one is carrier peering.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:32.310
For customers that need to
connect to the Google public

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:34.860
infrastructure-- and what
I mean by the Google public

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:40.440
infrastructure, that's our ASN
15169 and all of the public

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:41.870
Google services--

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:45.090
that's Search, that's G
Suite, that's YouTube,

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:46.920
and all the rest of it.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.650
The way you can connect to us
is either using direct peering

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:50.970
or carrier peering.

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:52.560
Direct peering is
where you meet us

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:56.190
in one of our peering locations,
and there are roughly about 100

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:57.930
all over the world.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:01.560
And you place a router there
and you peer with us using BGP.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.140
You need to meet our
peering requirements.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:06.580
And for many customers,
that's very difficult.

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:09.780
So we have another product
called carrier peering,

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.540
and that's when you work with
one of our service provider

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:15.480
partners-- and there's a
list on our website of them--

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:19.110
and allow them to peer
on your behalf with us.

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:21.450
A lot of customers use this.

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:24.120
Some use it for G Suite
connectivity, others

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.060
because they want real low
latency and fast access

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.290
to YouTube.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:30.480
I'm kidding.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.060
The other methods
for connecting are

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.370
VPN, which we're going to
talk about today, which

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:37.410
is a way of using
a cryptographically

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:42.060
secure method of connecting
over the internet to GCP

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.160
networks, your VPC and GCP.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:47.280
And then finally, dedicated
and partner interconnect.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.110
Dedicated and
partner interconnect

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:52.335
very much closely match
the direct peering

00:02:52.335 --> 00:02:54.300
and carrier pairing solutions.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.160
With dedicated interconnect,
you meet us in one of our POPs.

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:58.650
And with partner
interconnect, you

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:01.680
meet with one of our carriers,
which meets in one of our POPs.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:05.730
And I'll go into details
about both of those.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:08.520
So which one do I use?

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:10.800
This is a flowchart
that kind of tries

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.870
to help you make
those decisions.

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:17.070
By the way, a similar version
of this flowchart is available

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:20.100
on the
cloud.google.com/interconnect

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.050
site.

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:23.820
So for instance, if
you're using G Suite,

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:27.570
it sort of moves you to direct
peering or carrier peering.

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:29.250
If you don't need
a 10 gig pipe, it

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.500
tells you maybe you want to
use partnered interconnect

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.445
versus dedicated interconnect.

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:39.060
If you can't meet us at one
of our POPs, what do you do?

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:43.050
You do cloud VPN or the
partner interconnect solution.

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:45.135
And we'll get into all
the details about how

00:03:45.135 --> 00:03:47.280
all this stuff works promptly.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:52.840
So the first thing
we'd like to talk about

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:54.700
is how you use IPsec.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:04:02.780
IPsec is a low cost reasonable
bandwidth solution into GCP.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:05.819
So roughly, depending on how
you connect to us, if you're

00:04:05.819 --> 00:04:07.360
connecting to us
over a peering link,

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.400
you can get up to
3 gig per tunnel.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:13.700
Otherwise, it's about 1.5
gig large packet per tunnel.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:15.380
And if we go through
this diagram,

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:17.360
I want to point out
a couple of things.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:22.510
First of all, if you notice
there are two VPN tunnels here,

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:26.800
and they're connecting
using BGP to a Cloud Router.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:32.080
You don't have to use BGP,
you can use static routing,

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:34.630
but we recommend that
you use dynamic routing.

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:35.800
It's less error prone.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:38.060
It's easier to manage.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:39.250
But one of the things is--

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:42.700
you notice those BGP
sessions go to Cloud Router.

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:46.900
The Cloud Router is not
a traditional router.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:50.860
All it is, is the
BGP control plane.

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:55.180
And if you went to an earlier
talk that discussed Andromeda

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:57.460
and the underpinnings
of our network,

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:00.190
you'll understand that what
the Cloud Router is doing

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:05.230
is taking routes and programming
to our underlying SDN fabric.

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:07.480
And what it does is
it programs routes

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:11.200
into the VMs and your
VPC, so those VMs

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:13.720
know how to get off
of your network.

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:16.720
And what they have is that they
have a default route to the VPN

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:20.140
gateway which is managing
encryption and forwarding

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:24.070
that traffic on to your
on-premise network.

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:26.455
What that means is that--

00:05:26.455 --> 00:05:28.330
first of all, the Cloud
Router can support up

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:31.390
to 120 BGP sessions.

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:35.290
But it, obviously, can't support
128 VPNs going through it

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:39.730
because packets per second
would kind of overwhelm it.

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:42.430
So rather than worrying about
that we programmed these routes

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:45.160
directly into the
VMs, and all you

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:47.860
are then is limited
by the performance

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:50.830
of the VPN gateway and
each individual tunnel

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:53.020
which is about 1.5 gig.

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:55.510
But you can ECMP
that, and we have

00:05:55.510 --> 00:05:59.320
customers that are doing
20 gig of IPsec traffic

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:02.480
between their VPC and
on-premise network.

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:06.190
So it's a cool, very
scalable solution.

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:09.120
It offers an SLA.

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:11.280
And it does have some
drawbacks in that it's

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:12.970
going over the public internet.

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:17.135
So you are sort of
beholding to the latencies

00:06:17.135 --> 00:06:21.450
and jitter of a public
internet connection.

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:25.740
So many customers have used
this to start out with,

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:29.280
but eventually they move on
to our other interconnect

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:32.310
solutions which are dedicated
interconnect and partner

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:33.690
interconnect.

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:37.752
So dedicated interconnect, as
I said, it's a 10 gig solution.

00:06:37.752 --> 00:06:39.210
In other words,
all the connections

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:41.430
to Google with
dedicated interconnect

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:44.130
are supported a 10
gig per connection.

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:47.600
We support [? lag ?]
groups of up to eight,

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:49.350
so up to about 80 gig.

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:51.270
And then the partner
interconnect solution,

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:54.150
which will give you
sub-rate interfaces starting

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:56.940
at 50 megabits through
many partners, which

00:06:56.940 --> 00:06:58.150
I'll cover in just a second.

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:06.150
So let's talk a little
bit about the locations

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:09.850
around the dedicated
interconnect solution.

00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:11.930
First of all, to
connect to us, you

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:14.900
need to meet us in one of
our colocation facilities.

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:17.100
And I'll put a list of
the colocation facilities

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:18.900
up in just a second.

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:22.680
You are placing a peering
router in that facility,

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:25.830
and you're connecting to
us using cross-connects

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:27.840
to our peering edge.

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.447
And I distinctly
say a peering edge,

00:07:30.447 --> 00:07:31.530
because it's not a router.

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:32.410
It's just an edge.

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:37.680
It's a 10 gig port you plug in
and using our Espresso control

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:41.070
plane allows us to
package up your packets

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:42.991
and send them on to your VPC.

00:07:42.991 --> 00:07:45.240
By the way, if you don't
know anything about Espresso,

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:46.680
there's a great
paper on Espresso

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:50.280
that explains how we
do peering on our edge.

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:53.430
Very interesting not just
from an academic standpoint

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:56.610
but just on how you
build SDN fabrics.

00:07:56.610 --> 00:07:59.370
So to use dedicated
interconnect-- router

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:02.310
in one of our peering
locations and a cross-connect

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:04.160
to our peering edge.

00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:09.830
A little bit on requirements.

00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:14.420
So, first of all, it's 10
base LR, so just a comment.

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:16.340
We have had customers
that have come up

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:20.060
with other methods of connecting
to us, and those don't work.

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:23.840
We also support LA--
we require LACP.

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:26.300
The benefit of
LACP is as you want

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:29.960
to grow that infrastructure,
you can do it in a seamless way

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:33.580
by adding 10 gig connections
and growing that lag

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:35.669
bundle as you need to.

00:08:35.669 --> 00:08:41.074
So for customers that have very
large data rate requirements

00:08:41.074 --> 00:08:43.490
or are thinking they're going
to have very large data rate

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:47.690
requirements, it's really
important for LACP to be there.

00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:53.240
Support of 802.1q VLANs, which
most if not all routers support

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:55.160
that.

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:59.100
EBGP with multihop
is also required.

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:02.670
And finally, IPv4
link local addresses.

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:04.250
So all those are
the requirements

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:08.210
that you need for a dedicated
interconnect solution.

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:11.990
As part of the process, you
go into the cloud console,

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:13.100
you'd get an LOA.

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:16.490
You hand it over
to your data center

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:18.350
provider, whoever
that is, and they

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:19.770
would make that connection.

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:24.590
There's a very hold-your-hand
approach on setting up

00:09:24.590 --> 00:09:26.000
these connections.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:27.710
So after you get
the LOA, there's

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:29.970
instructions in
the email of what

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:33.680
to do next and take you step
by step through the process

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:35.570
of setting up that connection.

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:37.880
So we find it,
for the most part,

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:42.050
to be very non-error prone, lead
you through the whole process.

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:44.300
And we've had very,
very few escalations

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:46.890
from customers having a
problem through that process.

00:09:46.890 --> 00:09:50.270
So we try to hold your hand
if you're using this method.

00:09:52.870 --> 00:09:56.540
As far as interconnect
locations,

00:09:56.540 --> 00:09:59.330
this is what we have
today across the globe.

00:09:59.330 --> 00:10:01.240
So in North America--

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:03.940
a couple locations
in Canada and a host

00:10:03.940 --> 00:10:06.580
of locations throughout
the United States.

00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:08.930
One of the comments
I will make is that--

00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:12.550
and I don't have an overlay of
our data center fabric here--

00:10:12.550 --> 00:10:17.280
but when you may be concerned
about latency to VM workloads,

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:19.990
and if you're concerned about
latency to VM workloads,

00:10:19.990 --> 00:10:23.380
you want to be extremely
close to those VMs workloads.

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:25.510
So for instance, in
our Ashburn facility--

00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:30.420
that's the one that's
marked as Washington, DC--

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:35.560
you're roughly around a
millisecond away from a VM.

00:10:35.560 --> 00:10:37.780
Same thing with our
Los Angeles facility,

00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:41.230
same thing with Frankfurt,
London, and a few others--

00:10:41.230 --> 00:10:44.540
Tokyo and a few others
around the globe.

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:46.304
If you're really
concerned about latency--

00:10:46.304 --> 00:10:48.220
for instance, if you're
doing a lift and shift

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:50.590
workload, where you
have to leave some of it

00:10:50.590 --> 00:10:51.850
in your data center--

00:10:51.850 --> 00:10:56.350
so let's say you have a very
large DB2 in-memory database

00:10:56.350 --> 00:10:58.870
that you need to leave
in your data center.

00:10:58.870 --> 00:11:01.060
You might want to think
about those facilities

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:03.850
because you can take
that infrastructure

00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:06.400
and deploy in that facility
and still have low latency

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:09.110
access to your VM workloads.

00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:12.940
So just something to think about
when you go around selecting

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:15.320
a location to connect to us.

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:18.610
One thing I will say about
this list of locations.

00:11:18.610 --> 00:11:21.500
It's a evolving
list of locations.

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:23.080
So if you don't see
it up there, it's

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:25.870
probably going to be up there in
about another couple of months

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:27.500
or another half a
year, et cetera.

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:33.130
So we're slowly
rolling out locations

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:35.800
that support dedicated
interconnect over

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:36.557
the next year.

00:11:36.557 --> 00:11:38.890
So there will be a number of
additional locations there.

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:45.160
So now let's talk a
little bit about partner.

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:48.250
So the big difference
between dedicated and partner

00:11:48.250 --> 00:11:50.620
is that you're not meeting
us in one of our colocation

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:51.640
facilities.

00:11:51.640 --> 00:11:54.610
We have a number of partners
that actually have connections

00:11:54.610 --> 00:12:00.040
in our colocation facilities
and can provide you a Layer 2

00:12:00.040 --> 00:12:03.200
connection into that location.

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:07.240
So that means that
you're in your facility,

00:12:07.240 --> 00:12:11.140
and you're using one of
those partners to connect us.

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:12.980
There are different
kinds of partners

00:12:12.980 --> 00:12:16.190
that can help you connect.

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:18.514
So let's talk a
little bit about them.

00:12:18.514 --> 00:12:19.430
They're wave partners.

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:23.240
Wave partners are providing
you a physical optical cable

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:26.330
from your location
to a Google location.

00:12:26.330 --> 00:12:30.170
In that use case, you're
actually using the dedicated

00:12:30.170 --> 00:12:33.560
product, but you're using it
like if it was an extended--

00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:36.710
the optical fiber was like
an extended cross-connect.

00:12:36.710 --> 00:12:40.186
So you'd hand your LOA
to your wave partner,

00:12:40.186 --> 00:12:41.810
and they would figure
out where they're

00:12:41.810 --> 00:12:44.030
meeting us or you
tell them where

00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:45.560
you want to meet that fiber.

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:48.770
And they would hand it over to
whoever's providing the patch

00:12:48.770 --> 00:12:50.924
panel in that location.

00:12:50.924 --> 00:12:52.340
So there are a
number of customers

00:12:52.340 --> 00:12:55.570
that use that because
they want the bandwidth

00:12:55.570 --> 00:13:00.290
and the connections
that dedicated offers,

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:02.790
but they can't meet us in one
of our colocation facility.

00:13:02.790 --> 00:13:04.400
So they use a wave service.

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:08.000
The benefit of this is that you
don't have to place equipment

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:10.810
in the colocation facility.

00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:14.320
Another type of partner is
what we call Layer 2 partner.

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:18.244
They're either by EVPN
or a pseudowire partner.

00:13:18.244 --> 00:13:19.660
And what they're
doing is all they

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:23.470
provide you is a physical
connection that then you

00:13:23.470 --> 00:13:27.904
can do a Layer 3 VLAN over
that physical connection.

00:13:27.904 --> 00:13:29.320
So there are a
number of partners.

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:31.060
And if you go to our
website, we actually

00:13:31.060 --> 00:13:34.990
characterize partners that
the Layer 2 or Layer 3

00:13:34.990 --> 00:13:36.160
connectivity.

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:38.140
But those partners
are giving you

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:43.750
the ability to manage the VLANs
on your equipment on-premise.

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:47.200
And then finally there are Layer
3 partners or Layer 3 partner

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:51.230
MPLS VPN is a typical way
of talking about them.

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:55.630
They are typically doing
BGP on your behalf to us,

00:13:55.630 --> 00:13:58.610
and then they're doing
BGP to you as well.

00:13:58.610 --> 00:14:02.770
So they're managing how
you can connect to us.

00:14:02.770 --> 00:14:06.190
Typically, you're connecting
with an MPLS backbone,

00:14:06.190 --> 00:14:09.340
and you're telling them can you
please extend my MPLS backbone

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:10.810
to the cloud.

00:14:10.810 --> 00:14:14.500
So for those of you that
have an MPLS partner.

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:16.010
This is a great way to do it.

00:14:16.010 --> 00:14:18.400
So if you were at
a session yesterday

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:21.340
both AT&amp;T with their product
called NetBond and Verizon

00:14:21.340 --> 00:14:23.890
with their product
SCI were discussing

00:14:23.890 --> 00:14:27.028
how to do this Layer
3 type of connection.

00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:33.540
So what are some of
the other differences

00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:35.550
of partner and dedicated?

00:14:35.550 --> 00:14:38.130
I think the biggest difference
between partner and dedicated

00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:41.417
is that you can get sub-rate
interfaces with partner.

00:14:41.417 --> 00:14:43.500
So, for instance, if you're
a retail establishment

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:45.780
with many stores
all over the nation,

00:14:45.780 --> 00:14:48.030
you don't need a 10 gig
connection per store.

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:50.470
You may want to sub-rate
interface per store.

00:14:50.470 --> 00:14:52.580
You probably have
an MPLS provider.

00:14:52.580 --> 00:14:55.290
They're giving you
100 megabit MPLS

00:14:55.290 --> 00:14:57.940
linked to cloud for each
one of those stores.

00:14:57.940 --> 00:14:59.820
So that's something you
can get with partner,

00:14:59.820 --> 00:15:03.660
but you can't get with
the dedicated solution.

00:15:03.660 --> 00:15:05.690
Some of the other
big differences--

00:15:05.690 --> 00:15:08.690
because you're only
paying for what you need,

00:15:08.690 --> 00:15:10.050
it can be less expensive.

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:12.350
It can be less
expensive with partner,

00:15:12.350 --> 00:15:15.570
you don't have to pay for the
rack costs in a colocation

00:15:15.570 --> 00:15:16.100
location.

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:18.275
You don't have to think
about the amortized cost

00:15:18.275 --> 00:15:19.650
of the router that
you're putting

00:15:19.650 --> 00:15:21.150
in that location, et cetera.

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:24.710
So sometimes partner can be a
more cost effective solution.

00:15:24.710 --> 00:15:29.565
And as well with partner,
from start to finish--

00:15:29.565 --> 00:15:31.940
especially, if you already
have a partner and connections

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:35.750
to that partner can be extremely
fast, order of minutes.

00:15:35.750 --> 00:15:37.730
Whereas, if you're
using dedicated,

00:15:37.730 --> 00:15:41.120
unless you have the gear placed,
you have the rack bought,

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:43.190
you have all of those
contracts in place

00:15:43.190 --> 00:15:45.230
with the colocation
facility, that

00:15:45.230 --> 00:15:47.060
can take a little bit longer.

00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:50.640
Typically, dedicated takes
order a week versus partner

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:52.640
can take order minutes,
depending on the partner

00:15:52.640 --> 00:15:53.681
connection that you have.

00:15:57.490 --> 00:16:00.340
One other difference between
partner and dedicated

00:16:00.340 --> 00:16:03.940
is with dedicated you
own the 10 gig circuit.

00:16:03.940 --> 00:16:04.719
It's yours.

00:16:04.719 --> 00:16:06.260
You can do anything
you want with it.

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:09.750
You [? lend ?] VLANs
in multiple projects.

00:16:09.750 --> 00:16:13.270
And with a partner connection,
that 10 gig circuit

00:16:13.270 --> 00:16:18.220
is shared amongst a
number of other companies.

00:16:18.220 --> 00:16:23.320
We have provisions in place
so that that circuit is not

00:16:23.320 --> 00:16:24.620
overloaded.

00:16:24.620 --> 00:16:28.870
So we force our partners to
augment those circuits as they

00:16:28.870 --> 00:16:30.995
start reaching capacity levels.

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:36.990
So let's talk a little bit
about the partners we have.

00:16:36.990 --> 00:16:42.430
We launched the partner solution
about six to seven weeks ago.

00:16:42.430 --> 00:16:44.370
And we launched it
with a set of partners.

00:16:44.370 --> 00:16:49.080
Since then, we've added a
number of additional partners,

00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:50.010
and we continue.

00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:51.844
Actually, there were
a number of partners

00:16:51.844 --> 00:16:53.760
that would have been on
this list, except they

00:16:53.760 --> 00:16:56.850
didn't get the signature
on that contract in time.

00:16:56.850 --> 00:16:59.190
So they were left off
the list because I had

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:02.160
to do my slides at some point.

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:04.349
So this list continues to grow.

00:17:04.349 --> 00:17:06.337
If you don't see your
favorite partner here--

00:17:06.337 --> 00:17:07.170
come and talk to me.

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:10.390
We can get them on the list.

00:17:10.390 --> 00:17:11.970
And by the way,
if you want to see

00:17:11.970 --> 00:17:15.040
a full list of the partners,
there's a URL for that.

00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:16.710
In addition, at that
URL, it'll give you

00:17:16.710 --> 00:17:18.720
additional details
about the partner,

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:21.720
like how do you contact
them, what services they

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:24.589
offer, things like that.

00:17:24.589 --> 00:17:26.430
And I will say one thing--

00:17:26.430 --> 00:17:29.490
with this suite of
partners, this also

00:17:29.490 --> 00:17:31.800
extends the reach of the
number of data centers

00:17:31.800 --> 00:17:33.330
that we support.

00:17:33.330 --> 00:17:36.330
So if you look at
many of these partners

00:17:36.330 --> 00:17:39.030
and you go to this URL,
you'll see that they only

00:17:39.030 --> 00:17:41.190
meet us in certain locations.

00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:43.260
But for instance, if you go to--

00:17:43.260 --> 00:17:44.130
what's a good one?

00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:46.380
If you go to our
partner like a Megaport,

00:17:46.380 --> 00:17:49.260
they have connectivity
in a large number

00:17:49.260 --> 00:17:51.790
of facilities across the globe.

00:17:51.790 --> 00:17:55.090
So if you can't be in
one of our locations,

00:17:55.090 --> 00:17:56.910
many of these
partners can help you

00:17:56.910 --> 00:18:00.630
be in one of those locations
because they have a very

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:03.180
diverse set of connectivity.

00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:05.760
An example, I just said
Megaport-- another, Equinix,

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:07.830
for instance, has
their own backbone

00:18:07.830 --> 00:18:10.420
that connects all their
data centers together.

00:18:10.420 --> 00:18:12.300
So if you're in one
of their data centers,

00:18:12.300 --> 00:18:14.070
they can connect you to cloud.

00:18:14.070 --> 00:18:18.540
So many of these partners
give you that last mile,

00:18:18.540 --> 00:18:21.559
that extra data center
connectivity back into a cloud

00:18:21.559 --> 00:18:22.350
offering like GCPs.

00:18:27.310 --> 00:18:31.780
So let's talk a little bit about
the details of both partner

00:18:31.780 --> 00:18:33.370
and dedicated interconnect.

00:18:33.370 --> 00:18:36.460
I use a lot of the dedicated
interconnect diagrams here,

00:18:36.460 --> 00:18:41.120
but they also work for the
partner interconnect solution.

00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:43.660
So on your peering router,
whether it be on-premise

00:18:43.660 --> 00:18:45.100
or in one of our
data center-- one

00:18:45.100 --> 00:18:47.650
of our colocation
facilities, you're

00:18:47.650 --> 00:18:52.450
exposing a linked local
address to the Cloud router.

00:18:52.450 --> 00:18:54.940
We're exchanging BGP
across that link,

00:18:54.940 --> 00:19:00.550
and we're extending your
network into cloud VPC.

00:19:00.550 --> 00:19:04.670
That means that we take
routes from your network,

00:19:04.670 --> 00:19:07.480
and we program those
routes on the VMs.

00:19:07.480 --> 00:19:10.330
And we take those VMs
and expose those routes

00:19:10.330 --> 00:19:14.410
to your data center as well.

00:19:14.410 --> 00:19:18.360
So fully meshed-- it just looks
like you extended your data

00:19:18.360 --> 00:19:20.790
center into another room,
but what you've done

00:19:20.790 --> 00:19:23.310
is you've extended to the
cloud and in addition, you've

00:19:23.310 --> 00:19:27.940
extended to the globe as I'll
go through in just a second.

00:19:27.940 --> 00:19:30.870
So we have a
feature in the Cloud

00:19:30.870 --> 00:19:33.050
Router called global routing.

00:19:33.050 --> 00:19:34.830
And what global
routing does is it

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:39.180
takes your VPC subnets
that are in other regions

00:19:39.180 --> 00:19:43.590
and advertise them over a BGP
connection back onto your data

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:45.400
center.

00:19:45.400 --> 00:19:50.250
So if you see this diagram,
we have two connections.

00:19:50.250 --> 00:19:53.100
One in one region and
one in another region.

00:19:53.100 --> 00:19:56.370
Both of those connections are
advertising all the routes

00:19:56.370 --> 00:19:58.356
that you have on your VPC.

00:19:58.356 --> 00:20:00.810
The reason you might want
to do these in two regions

00:20:00.810 --> 00:20:03.780
is because you may have
a connection in Tokyo

00:20:03.780 --> 00:20:07.440
and a connection in Chicago,
but what's happening

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:09.930
is you're getting all
your routes to all

00:20:09.930 --> 00:20:12.210
your VPC over those two links.

00:20:12.210 --> 00:20:15.690
So you may have a VM running
in a data center in London,

00:20:15.690 --> 00:20:18.150
and you still have the access
to it from your on-prem,

00:20:18.150 --> 00:20:20.790
but you don't have a
connection in London.

00:20:20.790 --> 00:20:23.670
And the benefit is that
you're going over our backbone

00:20:23.670 --> 00:20:25.680
to get to those VMs.

00:20:25.680 --> 00:20:31.560
So as I said when I
started this presentation,

00:20:31.560 --> 00:20:35.218
you get access to that large
network with one connection.

00:20:38.360 --> 00:20:40.360
And this is a good
example of that.

00:20:40.360 --> 00:20:44.170
So in this use case, we're
connecting to one region

00:20:44.170 --> 00:20:47.670
and to one Cloud Router, but
because of global routing

00:20:47.670 --> 00:20:51.870
we're advertising the routes
to other regions over that one

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:53.420
link.

00:20:53.420 --> 00:20:56.457
So in this case, let's say
you're connected in Chicago,

00:20:56.457 --> 00:20:58.540
you still have access to
our data center in Tokyo.

00:20:58.540 --> 00:21:01.720
You have access to our
data center in London.

00:21:01.720 --> 00:21:04.210
You have access to our data
center in Frankfurt or Mumbai

00:21:04.210 --> 00:21:06.890
or wherever those
regions are set up.

00:21:06.890 --> 00:21:09.790
So it's a very
cost effective way

00:21:09.790 --> 00:21:13.240
of extending the reach of
your data center network

00:21:13.240 --> 00:21:17.190
to a global footprint.

00:21:17.190 --> 00:21:20.330
Now, I will caution there are
two charges when you do this.

00:21:20.330 --> 00:21:23.330
There is egress charges
from your local region.

00:21:23.330 --> 00:21:25.410
So, for instance, if you're
connected in Chicago,

00:21:25.410 --> 00:21:29.190
there'll be an egress charge
from a local region to Chicago

00:21:29.190 --> 00:21:30.067
out.

00:21:30.067 --> 00:21:31.650
And if you wanted
to connect to Tokyo,

00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:35.220
you're going to pay
a Tokyo to a local US

00:21:35.220 --> 00:21:38.890
region egress charges as well.

00:21:38.890 --> 00:21:40.890
But again, it's
cheaper than stringing

00:21:40.890 --> 00:21:43.389
a long, long connection
to a data center

00:21:43.389 --> 00:21:44.430
halfway around the world.

00:21:47.340 --> 00:21:51.680
So let's talk about how you
connect multiple projects.

00:21:51.680 --> 00:21:54.320
In the dedicated
use case, you'd have

00:21:54.320 --> 00:21:57.780
multiple VLANs over that
same 10 gig connection.

00:21:57.780 --> 00:22:00.170
In the partner use case, you
just have multiple VLANs,

00:22:00.170 --> 00:22:03.890
you don't care how they
route or who they route over.

00:22:03.890 --> 00:22:06.770
But what you're doing now
is with multiple projects,

00:22:06.770 --> 00:22:10.100
you have complete separation
between those two projects.

00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:13.280
You have network to assets
in those two projects.

00:22:13.280 --> 00:22:15.120
They don't know
one from one other,

00:22:15.120 --> 00:22:17.270
even if you're using the
dedicated single 10 gig

00:22:17.270 --> 00:22:18.050
connection.

00:22:18.050 --> 00:22:19.880
They can have overlapping
address space.

00:22:19.880 --> 00:22:21.900
They can do all
that kind of stuff.

00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:25.130
So this is one way of
connecting multiple projects.

00:22:25.130 --> 00:22:28.280
It is not the recommended
way, but some people

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:29.960
need to do it this way.

00:22:29.960 --> 00:22:35.180
The recommended way is to use
something called shared VPC.

00:22:35.180 --> 00:22:39.060
How many of you know
what shared VPC is?

00:22:39.060 --> 00:22:41.300
We're doing our job.

00:22:41.300 --> 00:22:44.520
What shared VPC
allows you to do is

00:22:44.520 --> 00:22:47.430
think of each individual
project the way

00:22:47.430 --> 00:22:50.130
you would think about
resources that you

00:22:50.130 --> 00:22:52.320
have in your data center.

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:54.730
So when you have a developer
come to you and say,

00:22:54.730 --> 00:22:58.230
I need VM resources,
I need network access.

00:22:58.230 --> 00:23:00.490
I have this special
project I'm working on.

00:23:00.490 --> 00:23:02.490
You don't care what the
project is, but you need

00:23:02.490 --> 00:23:04.300
to give them network access--

00:23:04.300 --> 00:23:06.180
that's what shared VPC is doing.

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:08.970
It lets you say I control
the network resources.

00:23:08.970 --> 00:23:10.920
I control the firewall rules.

00:23:10.920 --> 00:23:14.730
And I give you access based
on what your needs are.

00:23:14.730 --> 00:23:20.580
Do you need access back to
your data center database?

00:23:20.580 --> 00:23:23.790
Probably not, so you
firewall that stuff off.

00:23:23.790 --> 00:23:26.820
The shared VPC allows you
to control who gets access

00:23:26.820 --> 00:23:29.550
to the network, what
firewall rules are placed

00:23:29.550 --> 00:23:31.510
on those individual projects.

00:23:31.510 --> 00:23:37.200
So this is a way of using your
network assets in the most

00:23:37.200 --> 00:23:39.360
efficient way possible.

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:43.770
So I have customers come to
me and say, I need 100 VLANs.

00:23:43.770 --> 00:23:45.110
Well, why do you need 100 VLANs?

00:23:45.110 --> 00:23:47.550
Well, I have 100 projects
I need to connect back

00:23:47.550 --> 00:23:50.900
to my data center.

00:23:50.900 --> 00:23:53.360
If you think about that,
what they really want to do

00:23:53.360 --> 00:23:55.310
is they want to use shared VPC.

00:23:55.310 --> 00:23:57.050
Because they don't
need 100 VLANs,

00:23:57.050 --> 00:24:00.950
they want to get
networking to 100 projects.

00:24:00.950 --> 00:24:02.590
And this is an efficient way--

00:24:02.590 --> 00:24:04.670
actually, I got the
scaling numbers wrong.

00:24:04.670 --> 00:24:07.910
But this is an efficient
way of giving access

00:24:07.910 --> 00:24:12.210
to a number of projects
using one VLAN resource.

00:24:12.210 --> 00:24:15.230
So you can put in a
high capacity VLAN

00:24:15.230 --> 00:24:17.630
and give access to
a number of projects

00:24:17.630 --> 00:24:19.930
and control the way they
access your data center.

00:24:22.159 --> 00:24:24.450
And by the way, there was a
great talk on this that you

00:24:24.450 --> 00:24:26.730
should listen to because this
[? already ?] [? happened. ?]

00:24:26.730 --> 00:24:28.771
So it'll be available on
YouTube in a week or so.

00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:35.370
So let's talk about
connecting to multiple clouds.

00:24:35.370 --> 00:24:37.560
Because I don't
care who you are,

00:24:37.560 --> 00:24:40.080
you most likely have
some resources that

00:24:40.080 --> 00:24:42.450
are running in another cloud.

00:24:42.450 --> 00:24:43.930
And how do you do that?

00:24:43.930 --> 00:24:46.140
Well, there are a couple
methods of doing that.

00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:50.910
What I'm showing here is showing
how you would use a router that

00:24:50.910 --> 00:24:54.330
you have in a colocation
facility or on-premise,

00:24:54.330 --> 00:24:55.740
and you'd take two legs--

00:24:55.740 --> 00:24:58.950
stick one in one
cloud service provider

00:24:58.950 --> 00:25:00.960
and stick the other one in GCP.

00:25:00.960 --> 00:25:03.750
You'd get route information
from both of those,

00:25:03.750 --> 00:25:07.260
and now you have a connection
between those resources

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:10.200
and to cloud infrastructures.

00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:12.000
The one thing you
have to remember

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:15.780
is you are you could have an
issue with overlapping IPs,

00:25:15.780 --> 00:25:18.450
you need to be careful
of that in that you

00:25:18.450 --> 00:25:22.050
can't have overlapping IPs
without having a NAT device

00:25:22.050 --> 00:25:24.030
on-premise.

00:25:24.030 --> 00:25:26.190
Now, you say, wow,
that's a real pain.

00:25:26.190 --> 00:25:28.530
I have to go stick a
router in some point

00:25:28.530 --> 00:25:31.451
[INAUDIBLE] someplace and
connect this and manage

00:25:31.451 --> 00:25:31.950
all that.

00:25:31.950 --> 00:25:35.550
That's a real over-the-top
amount of effort.

00:25:35.550 --> 00:25:38.160
There are companies-- and
I'll mention Megaport here

00:25:38.160 --> 00:25:40.410
because their
product is actually

00:25:40.410 --> 00:25:42.630
in production right now.

00:25:42.630 --> 00:25:46.200
And there's another large
infrastructure provider

00:25:46.200 --> 00:25:48.820
that is actually doing
something very similar.

00:25:48.820 --> 00:25:50.670
What they've done is
they've virtualized

00:25:50.670 --> 00:25:53.490
the routing function
in those POPs.

00:25:53.490 --> 00:25:57.270
And they give you
APIs or user interface

00:25:57.270 --> 00:26:00.150
that allow you to point
and click and connect

00:26:00.150 --> 00:26:02.130
two CSPs together.

00:26:02.130 --> 00:26:07.530
So a very easy to use,
very cloud-like approach

00:26:07.530 --> 00:26:10.740
to connecting to cloud
service routers together.

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:13.530
So the Megaport solution is
called the Megaport Cloud

00:26:13.530 --> 00:26:15.450
Router, it's on their website.

00:26:15.450 --> 00:26:18.270
You can trial it, it has a
pretty nice user interface

00:26:18.270 --> 00:26:19.500
to walk through.

00:26:19.500 --> 00:26:22.540
They have a booth
on site too as well.

00:26:22.540 --> 00:26:26.370
The other, and I can't speak
for the other company that's

00:26:26.370 --> 00:26:28.680
doing this because
I'm not authorized to,

00:26:28.680 --> 00:26:32.580
but I'm sure you'll
see press when they

00:26:32.580 --> 00:26:35.460
make their solution available.

00:26:35.460 --> 00:26:38.040
So that's how you'd
connect two cloud service

00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:39.210
providers together.

00:26:39.210 --> 00:26:42.690
There's actually another
method of doing this as well.

00:26:42.690 --> 00:26:44.970
Because one thing
you can do is just

00:26:44.970 --> 00:26:48.730
connect them using
an IPsec connection.

00:26:48.730 --> 00:26:52.200
So the cloud VPN solution,
there are a number

00:26:52.200 --> 00:26:57.360
of guides that help you
connect to common cloud service

00:26:57.360 --> 00:26:59.400
provider infrastructure.

00:26:59.400 --> 00:27:01.474
And you can make an
IPsec connection,

00:27:01.474 --> 00:27:02.640
you can have multiple links.

00:27:02.640 --> 00:27:05.070
You can do ECMP over
those multiple links

00:27:05.070 --> 00:27:06.930
and get pretty
reasonable connectivity

00:27:06.930 --> 00:27:10.860
between GCP and another
cloud service provider.

00:27:10.860 --> 00:27:14.070
Google as a whole has
very good, I would say,

00:27:14.070 --> 00:27:20.640
rich connectivity peering
between our CSP competitors.

00:27:20.640 --> 00:27:24.630
So you can be assured
that those links are going

00:27:24.630 --> 00:27:27.626
over adequate connectivity.

00:27:31.600 --> 00:27:35.580
So let's talk a little bit
about high availability

00:27:35.580 --> 00:27:38.370
because your connections from
your data center to the cloud

00:27:38.370 --> 00:27:41.310
really need to have some
level of reliability

00:27:41.310 --> 00:27:43.590
when you can connect.

00:27:43.590 --> 00:27:44.970
So to do a--

00:27:44.970 --> 00:27:50.220
we offer two SLA'ed levels
with the dedicated and partner

00:27:50.220 --> 00:27:52.230
interconnect solutions.

00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:54.960
Again, it doesn't matter
which one you're using,

00:27:54.960 --> 00:27:58.690
you have to do more or less
exactly the same thing.

00:27:58.690 --> 00:28:02.070
You have to meet us in
one metro with two links

00:28:02.070 --> 00:28:04.770
to get a three nines
of availability.

00:28:04.770 --> 00:28:08.610
What I mean by a metro is if
you remember that map with all

00:28:08.610 --> 00:28:10.740
the points of presence,
a metro is one

00:28:10.740 --> 00:28:12.630
of those points of presence.

00:28:12.630 --> 00:28:16.020
So you need to meet us in one
of those points of presence

00:28:16.020 --> 00:28:18.470
with two connections.

00:28:18.470 --> 00:28:20.160
If you're using
dedicated, that means

00:28:20.160 --> 00:28:22.050
you have two 10 gig links.

00:28:22.050 --> 00:28:23.580
If you're using a
partner solution,

00:28:23.580 --> 00:28:28.560
that means you have two VLANs
connected in that metro.

00:28:28.560 --> 00:28:31.620
That will give you a
three nines of SLA.

00:28:31.620 --> 00:28:34.380
We also recommend that you use
Graceful Restart on the Cloud

00:28:34.380 --> 00:28:38.400
Router, and I'll go into some
details of that in just a bit.

00:28:38.400 --> 00:28:41.220
To get to four nines
of availability,

00:28:41.220 --> 00:28:44.760
you need to meet us in two
metros with two connections

00:28:44.760 --> 00:28:46.650
in each one of those metros.

00:28:46.650 --> 00:28:49.170
So let's say you're
meeting us in Chicago,

00:28:49.170 --> 00:28:52.170
you also need to meet us
in Dallas or Los Angeles

00:28:52.170 --> 00:28:55.860
or somewhere else to get the
four nines of availability.

00:28:55.860 --> 00:29:00.690
And again, I stress that this is
two metros because our SLAs are

00:29:00.690 --> 00:29:04.140
metro bound, and they're not
four connections anywhere

00:29:04.140 --> 00:29:06.340
you want.

00:29:06.340 --> 00:29:09.420
So this is a way of
getting a four nines of SLA

00:29:09.420 --> 00:29:11.580
into the Google infrastructure.

00:29:11.580 --> 00:29:14.930
In addition, with the
four nines of SLA,

00:29:14.930 --> 00:29:18.510
you need to implement, global
routing on the Cloud Router

00:29:18.510 --> 00:29:21.825
so that I can advertise all
the routes over those links.

00:29:21.825 --> 00:29:24.570
So if one link fails, I
can use the other link

00:29:24.570 --> 00:29:25.990
to connect to GCP.

00:29:31.860 --> 00:29:34.610
So we talked about
IPsec as being

00:29:34.610 --> 00:29:39.380
a method of connecting back
into the Google infrastructure

00:29:39.380 --> 00:29:40.790
as a way to start out.

00:29:40.790 --> 00:29:42.849
You're not sure
you want to do this

00:29:42.849 --> 00:29:44.890
or you don't know what
the volume of connectivity

00:29:44.890 --> 00:29:48.120
is or you're just in
a proof of concept--

00:29:48.120 --> 00:29:52.010
so you can start with an IPsec
connection to connect to us.

00:29:52.010 --> 00:29:55.850
The default-- by
the way, I recommend

00:29:55.850 --> 00:29:57.080
using dynamic routing.

00:29:57.080 --> 00:29:59.240
This particular use
case is all going

00:29:59.240 --> 00:30:01.160
to be using dynamic routing.

00:30:01.160 --> 00:30:05.270
And in this case, you're
advertising all your BGP routes

00:30:05.270 --> 00:30:10.850
using a default MED of 100.

00:30:10.850 --> 00:30:14.920
But now you want to move over
to a dedicated or partner

00:30:14.920 --> 00:30:21.320
interconnect, so you've set up
two VLANs or two dedicated 10

00:30:21.320 --> 00:30:23.510
gig links with VLANs over them.

00:30:23.510 --> 00:30:27.320
And you've set a default
MED priority of 1,000.

00:30:27.320 --> 00:30:30.920
What that means is that
your IPsec connection is

00:30:30.920 --> 00:30:36.050
the preferred path to and
from your data center.

00:30:36.050 --> 00:30:40.630
And the secondary path is
that dedicated interconnect.

00:30:40.630 --> 00:30:44.830
And you can go through the
CLI or you can use the GUI,

00:30:44.830 --> 00:30:49.210
and you can change the MED
values and traffic immediately

00:30:49.210 --> 00:30:52.970
swings over to the preferred
method that you've chosen.

00:30:52.970 --> 00:30:55.570
So in this use case, you've
moved all your traffic

00:30:55.570 --> 00:30:57.400
over the VPN, and
you've moved it

00:30:57.400 --> 00:31:00.640
on to the new dedicated
interconnect that you set up.

00:31:00.640 --> 00:31:03.520
One of the benefits here is
also you can still maintain

00:31:03.520 --> 00:31:06.650
that IPsec connectivity.

00:31:06.650 --> 00:31:09.120
So if you have a problem with
the dedicated interconnect,

00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:12.350
it'll all swing back
to your VPNs as well.

00:31:12.350 --> 00:31:15.100
So a nice very
simple way of doing

00:31:15.100 --> 00:31:18.905
both the transition and a
failover mechanism to the VPN

00:31:18.905 --> 00:31:19.405
tunnels.

00:31:22.460 --> 00:31:25.190
So we talked a little bit
about Graceful Restart as a way

00:31:25.190 --> 00:31:27.410
to minimize downtime.

00:31:27.410 --> 00:31:29.600
Cloud Router's a
software component,

00:31:29.600 --> 00:31:31.850
we update it all the time.

00:31:31.850 --> 00:31:34.590
Like, once a week, we're
doing some change to it,

00:31:34.590 --> 00:31:36.320
updating that software.

00:31:36.320 --> 00:31:38.990
And you will have an issue
because your BGP session

00:31:38.990 --> 00:31:39.820
goes down.

00:31:39.820 --> 00:31:42.950
BGP session goes down, you lose
connectivity, withdraw routes,

00:31:42.950 --> 00:31:44.940
do all that kind of stuff.

00:31:44.940 --> 00:31:48.410
So Graceful Restart is a way
of not withdrawing routes when

00:31:48.410 --> 00:31:50.570
you have a BGP session down.

00:31:50.570 --> 00:31:52.820
And it gives you the
ability to say wait

00:31:52.820 --> 00:31:55.670
for a little bit of time
before you withdraw routes.

00:31:55.670 --> 00:31:58.610
That BGP session might
be coming back up,

00:31:58.610 --> 00:32:02.450
and I want to be assured that
I still have connectivity.

00:32:02.450 --> 00:32:05.450
Our recommendation is
to-- and by the way,

00:32:05.450 --> 00:32:11.270
there is a lot of documentation
on this on our website as well

00:32:11.270 --> 00:32:15.980
as [? they're the ?] sample CLIs
for both Juniper and Cisco gear

00:32:15.980 --> 00:32:19.070
on how you set all of
this up to take advantage

00:32:19.070 --> 00:32:21.990
of our recommended
configuration.

00:32:21.990 --> 00:32:26.830
And the recommendation is
to turn on Graceful Restart

00:32:26.830 --> 00:32:32.120
but do not use any of the
modifiers for Graceful Restart.

00:32:32.120 --> 00:32:34.520
And what that will do
is it will give you

00:32:34.520 --> 00:32:40.190
a connection that has about
61 seconds of failover time.

00:32:40.190 --> 00:32:44.630
That means if your
BGP session went down

00:32:44.630 --> 00:32:47.660
because of an actual a physical
cut in cables or something

00:32:47.660 --> 00:32:52.400
like that, it'll take about 61
seconds for that BPG traffic

00:32:52.400 --> 00:32:55.370
to failover to the
secondary like.

00:32:55.370 --> 00:32:57.410
We're going to improve
the timing on this

00:32:57.410 --> 00:33:00.740
through implementing some
other methods that you

00:33:00.740 --> 00:33:04.010
find in common
off-the-shelf routers.

00:33:04.010 --> 00:33:05.990
And those of you who
know things like BFD,

00:33:05.990 --> 00:33:10.630
and we'll improve this
failover time over time.

00:33:10.630 --> 00:33:15.290
But this today is the default
recommended configuration,

00:33:15.290 --> 00:33:18.170
and this will give you a
61 second failover time.

00:33:21.160 --> 00:33:23.230
We also talked a lot
about a global routing,

00:33:23.230 --> 00:33:25.170
and I want to go a little
bit under the covers

00:33:25.170 --> 00:33:26.940
on how global routing
works and what

00:33:26.940 --> 00:33:29.530
you get out of global routing.

00:33:29.530 --> 00:33:33.000
So when you set up
global routing that means

00:33:33.000 --> 00:33:38.020
all the subnets are advertised
over your BGP session.

00:33:38.020 --> 00:33:41.280
If you have multiple
connections to GCP,

00:33:41.280 --> 00:33:44.796
you want to make sure that
you're using the optimal path.

00:33:44.796 --> 00:33:46.920
So, for instance, if you
have a connection in Tokyo

00:33:46.920 --> 00:33:50.250
and one in Chicago, you don't
want your data center in Tokyo

00:33:50.250 --> 00:33:53.640
to go over your backbone
and come into Chicago.

00:33:53.640 --> 00:33:56.730
So what we do is on
the advertisement,

00:33:56.730 --> 00:34:01.110
we modify the MEDs
of the remote subnets

00:34:01.110 --> 00:34:05.970
by adding the RTT value
of those remote subnets.

00:34:05.970 --> 00:34:09.960
So your subnets in Tokyo
will have a very low MED

00:34:09.960 --> 00:34:14.130
value in Tokyo, and they'll
have a very high MED value

00:34:14.130 --> 00:34:15.420
in Chicago.

00:34:15.420 --> 00:34:19.110
So traffic from Tokyo will
prefer your Tokyo connections

00:34:19.110 --> 00:34:23.929
and traffic from Chicago would
prefer the Chicago connection.

00:34:23.929 --> 00:34:27.860
The one comment I will make
is that you should make sure

00:34:27.860 --> 00:34:32.179
that the default MED values
are far enough apart,

00:34:32.179 --> 00:34:34.730
so you don't run into an issue.

00:34:34.730 --> 00:34:37.880
For instance, if you
were to default of 100,

00:34:37.880 --> 00:34:41.909
and let's say you're doing
Chicago and New York,

00:34:41.909 --> 00:34:44.089
you may have some interesting--

00:34:44.089 --> 00:34:46.130
If you want to prefer your
Chicago connection all

00:34:46.130 --> 00:34:49.219
the time, add 1,000
to the default value

00:34:49.219 --> 00:34:51.590
that you're advertising
out of New York

00:34:51.590 --> 00:34:53.090
so that you don't
get into an issue

00:34:53.090 --> 00:34:57.890
where the round trip time is
longer than the default MED

00:34:57.890 --> 00:34:59.630
value that you're advertising.

00:34:59.630 --> 00:35:03.300
So that will always prefer
one connection over the other

00:35:03.300 --> 00:35:05.330
if you add a very
large amount of value

00:35:05.330 --> 00:35:07.220
to the default MED value.

00:35:07.220 --> 00:35:09.350
And all of these
things are available

00:35:09.350 --> 00:35:12.560
in the Cloud Router
CLI as well as

00:35:12.560 --> 00:35:15.680
in the GUI to be able
to set all of that up.

00:35:20.660 --> 00:35:24.590
Monitoring-- so now you have all
this great infrastructure set

00:35:24.590 --> 00:35:27.440
up and you everything
is working great,

00:35:27.440 --> 00:35:29.720
how do you monitor
this infrastructure?

00:35:29.720 --> 00:35:32.510
There are two distinct ways of
monitoring this infrastructure.

00:35:32.510 --> 00:35:35.420
One of them is you can go
into the Cloud Console,

00:35:35.420 --> 00:35:39.590
and we will give you things
like link utilization,

00:35:39.590 --> 00:35:41.210
drop data, et cetera.

00:35:41.210 --> 00:35:42.810
And those are time
series values,

00:35:42.810 --> 00:35:44.226
so you can select
whether you want

00:35:44.226 --> 00:35:47.960
to see the last hour or the last
day or last week, et cetera.

00:35:47.960 --> 00:35:51.110
So pretty nice,
rich user interface

00:35:51.110 --> 00:35:53.060
around all the
monitoring data, and you

00:35:53.060 --> 00:35:55.940
can get that right out
of the Cloud Console

00:35:55.940 --> 00:36:00.110
in the section around the
object you're trying to monitor.

00:36:00.110 --> 00:36:02.060
So you can go into
the interconnect

00:36:02.060 --> 00:36:03.800
and monitor that interconnect.

00:36:03.800 --> 00:36:07.550
With partner, you can go to
the VLAN and monitor that VLAN.

00:36:07.550 --> 00:36:11.460
In addition to that, we also
support Stackdriver monitoring.

00:36:11.460 --> 00:36:14.600
So, for instance, if you
want to put alerts on--

00:36:14.600 --> 00:36:16.490
if you're using
dedicated interconnect

00:36:16.490 --> 00:36:23.340
and you want to put alerts
on the DB value associated

00:36:23.340 --> 00:36:25.410
with the connection--

00:36:25.410 --> 00:36:27.850
what's the light levels on
a particular connection.

00:36:27.850 --> 00:36:29.530
And if they drop
below a certain level

00:36:29.530 --> 00:36:31.230
and you want to be
alerted, you can

00:36:31.230 --> 00:36:32.670
do all of that in Stackdriver.

00:36:32.670 --> 00:36:36.090
If you want to do an alert that
says my link utilization's up

00:36:36.090 --> 00:36:39.120
over 75%, you can do
that into Stackdriver

00:36:39.120 --> 00:36:42.960
and get those alerts so that
you know what's going on.

00:36:42.960 --> 00:36:44.340
While we're talking
about alerts,

00:36:44.340 --> 00:36:46.560
let me mention one other thing.

00:36:46.560 --> 00:36:49.110
We take down infrastructure
from time to time--

00:36:49.110 --> 00:36:50.370
mainly for maintenance.

00:36:50.370 --> 00:36:52.380
We're putting a new
line card, we're

00:36:52.380 --> 00:36:54.900
readdressing some
ports, stuff like that.

00:36:54.900 --> 00:36:58.530
All of that information is also
available in the Cloud Console.

00:36:58.530 --> 00:37:01.140
You can get alerts on when
we do maintenance outages.

00:37:01.140 --> 00:37:02.600
So that you're aware--

00:37:02.600 --> 00:37:05.140
your BGP sessions go
down, it's not an issue.

00:37:05.140 --> 00:37:08.130
You know that's a maintenance
window, you've done an outage.

00:37:08.130 --> 00:37:12.610
We try to notify you within
a reasonable amount of time.

00:37:12.610 --> 00:37:14.970
So that if you want to, for
instance, swing your traffic

00:37:14.970 --> 00:37:17.700
away from one link to another
because of that maintenance

00:37:17.700 --> 00:37:19.450
event, you can do that.

00:37:19.450 --> 00:37:22.530
So all of that is available
in the console, all of this

00:37:22.530 --> 00:37:23.700
is documented as well.

00:37:23.700 --> 00:37:26.770
So I do recommend reading
the documentation.

00:37:26.770 --> 00:37:29.426
I'm always surprised at
how many customers always

00:37:29.426 --> 00:37:31.050
do a Google search
before they actually

00:37:31.050 --> 00:37:33.420
go read the documentation,
and the Google search just

00:37:33.420 --> 00:37:35.340
takes them back to
the documentation.

00:37:35.340 --> 00:37:38.390
So a good way to figure out
how these products work--

00:37:38.390 --> 00:37:41.040
if there's not a lot of
documentation, there's enough.

00:37:41.040 --> 00:37:43.230
It's not a long read
take, take advantage

00:37:43.230 --> 00:37:46.710
of the time and effort our
Doc people go into putting out

00:37:46.710 --> 00:37:49.110
a really stellar amount
of documentation that

00:37:49.110 --> 00:37:52.920
helps you understand how
all this stuff works.

00:37:52.920 --> 00:37:55.050
So finally, let's
talk a little bit

00:37:55.050 --> 00:37:59.040
about how much all
of this stuff costs.

00:37:59.040 --> 00:38:03.810
So VPN, we charge
$0.05 per tunnel hour.

00:38:03.810 --> 00:38:07.790
That works out I believe $32
a month for a VPN tunnel.

00:38:07.790 --> 00:38:09.770
With dedicated
interconnect, it's

00:38:09.770 --> 00:38:13.960
$1,700 for a 10 gig
circuit per month.

00:38:13.960 --> 00:38:16.735
And then for every VLAN that
you put on that 10 gig circuit,

00:38:16.735 --> 00:38:20.490
it's a charge of $72.

00:38:20.490 --> 00:38:23.130
If you're thinking
about redundancy,

00:38:23.130 --> 00:38:26.490
three nines would mean two
connections, so $3,400 a month.

00:38:26.490 --> 00:38:29.550
And then, of course, with
a four nines of redundancy,

00:38:29.550 --> 00:38:37.470
you're doubling that
again for $6,800 a month.

00:38:37.470 --> 00:38:41.350
On the partner interconnect,
it's a sliding scale.

00:38:41.350 --> 00:38:45.390
It starts at about $39 a month
for a 50 megabit attachment

00:38:45.390 --> 00:38:51.410
and goes all the way up to
$1,700 for a 10 gig attachment.

00:38:51.410 --> 00:38:54.040
Our peering solutions are free--

00:38:54.040 --> 00:38:58.240
both direct peering solutions
free, our carrier peering

00:38:58.240 --> 00:38:59.801
solution is free.

00:38:59.801 --> 00:39:01.300
Of course, you may
have to be paying

00:39:01.300 --> 00:39:02.800
some money to whoever
the carrier is

00:39:02.800 --> 00:39:04.450
that's helping you peer.

00:39:04.450 --> 00:39:09.040
But we have a settlement
free peering solution.

00:39:09.040 --> 00:39:12.190
Of course, you do pay
egress costs on those links

00:39:12.190 --> 00:39:17.790
and those costs are [? our ?]
internet egress cost rate.

00:39:17.790 --> 00:39:22.500
Speaking of egress
on VPN, on VPN,

00:39:22.500 --> 00:39:24.780
it's just like an
internet connection.

00:39:24.780 --> 00:39:26.970
So depending on how
you're connecting to us

00:39:26.970 --> 00:39:28.560
and where you're
connecting to us,

00:39:28.560 --> 00:39:33.060
that's runs from about $0.08 a
gig all the way to $0.23 a gig.

00:39:33.060 --> 00:39:34.920
And the higher end
of the scale is

00:39:34.920 --> 00:39:38.040
for places like Australia
and South America,

00:39:38.040 --> 00:39:41.910
where it costs us a lot
more to provide transit

00:39:41.910 --> 00:39:43.830
in those locations.

00:39:43.830 --> 00:39:47.160
For dedicated and partner
interconnect in North America,

00:39:47.160 --> 00:39:48.870
we charge $0.02 per gig.

00:39:48.870 --> 00:39:50.670
If you're interested
in other locales,

00:39:50.670 --> 00:39:52.830
please go to the website,
and all that pricing

00:39:52.830 --> 00:39:54.750
is available there as well.

00:39:54.750 --> 00:39:56.670
For carrier and
direct peering, we

00:39:56.670 --> 00:39:59.700
do give a discount on
our standard internet

00:39:59.700 --> 00:40:03.060
rate of egress, and in
North America, that's

00:40:03.060 --> 00:40:09.580
$0.05 per gig for both
carrier and direct peering.

00:40:09.580 --> 00:40:17.390
So we gave a lot of
sessions at this conference.

00:40:17.390 --> 00:40:19.910
And this is the list of all
the sessions that are here.

00:40:19.910 --> 00:40:22.970
The reason I'm putting this
up is all these sessions

00:40:22.970 --> 00:40:24.570
will be available on YouTube.

00:40:24.570 --> 00:40:27.572
So if you miss something that's
of crucial interest to you--

00:40:27.572 --> 00:40:29.405
like, I think there's
actually one happening

00:40:29.405 --> 00:40:31.050
at exactly the same time.

00:40:31.050 --> 00:40:33.740
So if you were worrying
enterprise ready networking

00:40:33.740 --> 00:40:36.890
for GKE, which is happening
at exactly the same time slot,

00:40:36.890 --> 00:40:40.220
go to YouTube, and you'll be
able to get that presentation

00:40:40.220 --> 00:40:42.180
online.

00:40:42.180 --> 00:40:45.186
And two final
things, I don't know

00:40:45.186 --> 00:40:46.310
if you guys have seen this.

00:40:46.310 --> 00:40:48.650
We have a wonderful poster.

00:40:48.650 --> 00:40:49.892
It's an online giveaway.

00:40:49.892 --> 00:40:51.350
In other words,
you go to this site

00:40:51.350 --> 00:40:53.180
and either you can
download it yourself

00:40:53.180 --> 00:40:55.380
or you can have
it mailed to you.

00:40:55.380 --> 00:40:58.850
But this is a poster
that shows our network,

00:40:58.850 --> 00:41:02.120
all our POP locations, all
our data center locations,

00:41:02.120 --> 00:41:05.000
all our undersea cables, all
our terrestrial cabling, et

00:41:05.000 --> 00:41:05.540
cetera--

00:41:05.540 --> 00:41:07.360
all in one beautiful picture.

00:41:07.360 --> 00:41:09.470
So it's kind of cool--

00:41:09.470 --> 00:41:11.870
put up on your wall and
people will ask you,

00:41:11.870 --> 00:41:14.660
what's that map of
the world all about?

00:41:14.660 --> 00:41:18.590
Finally, in closing, I'd like,
if you could, please give

00:41:18.590 --> 00:41:20.100
feedback on this session.

00:41:20.100 --> 00:41:26.215
You'll find feedback in the
Next '18 application under--

00:41:26.215 --> 00:41:27.770
